        title: 'Acute Proximal Weakness — Sick Check First',
        body: '**True acute neuromuscular weakness is an airway/breathing emergency until proven otherwise.** The muscles that fail to lift an arm are the same ones (diaphragm, intercostals, bulbar muscles) that fail to breathe and protect the airway. Do not anchor on "just weak" — a patient can talk, look comfortable, and be minutes from respiratory arrest.\n\n**⚠️ 5 DO-NOT-MISS diagnoses**\n1. **Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS)** — ascending, symmetric, areflexic weakness with progression to respiratory failure and autonomic instability.\n2. **Myasthenic crisis** — fatigable weakness, diplopia, ptosis, bulbar/respiratory failure; can be precipitated by infection or drugs.\n3. **Botulism** — descending, symmetric, bulbar-first weakness with dilated/fixed pupils, dry mouth, and no fever/sensory loss.\n4. **Tick paralysis** — ascending flaccid paralysis with areflexia that reverses when an attached tick is found and removed.\n5. **Periodic paralysis / severe dyskalemia** — flaccid weakness from hypo- or hyperkalemia; the ECG and potassium can be immediately lethal.\n\n*Also on this hub:* **Lambert-Eaton (LEMS)** — the classic *proximal* NMJ disease (legs > arms), strength **improves with use**, dry mouth, ~50-60% paraneoplastic (small cell lung cancer); and **spinal cord / cauda equina compression**, which masquerades as "can\'t lift."\n\n**First 60 seconds:** assess **airway/breathing** (single-breath count, respiratory rate, use of accessory muscles, secretions/swallow), get a **bedside NIF and vital capacity** early, check reflexes and the pattern (ascending vs descending), pupils, and a **stat potassium + ECG**. **Deteriorating respiratory mechanics → intubate; do NOT wait for a blood gas to declare failure.**\n\n**Single-breath count (no equipment needed):** patient takes a maximal breath and counts out loud at a normal cadence — **<20 is concerning, <10 signals imminent respiratory failure.**\n\n**⚠️ If you intubate: avoid succinylcholine.** Subacute denervation (GBS, botulism, tick paralysis, spinal cord injury, prolonged immobility/critical illness) up-regulates extrajunctional and α7 acetylcholine receptors across the whole muscle membrane; succinylcholine then depolarizes all of them and can cause **hyperkalemic cardiac arrest**. Risk is generally taken to begin ~**48-72 h** after the insult and to persist for months — and it has been reported in GBS *after* clinical recovery. **Use rocuronium.** (Myasthenia gravis is the exception — receptors are *down*-regulated, so MG patients are succinylcholine-*resistant* and markedly *sensitive* to non-depolarizers.)',
