        body: '**Myopathic proximal weakness** (manage here; use the deep-dive when rhabdo is confirmed):\n\n- **Check CK, potassium, calcium, phosphate, renal function, and urinalysis.** A markedly elevated CK with pigmenturia → [Rhabdomyolysis](#/tree/rhabdomyolysis) — aggressive IV fluids and monitor for hyperkalemia and AKI.\n- **Correct electrolyte disturbances** driving the weakness (potassium, calcium, phosphate, magnesium).\n- **Medication review:** statins, steroids, colchicine; stop the offender.\n- **Inflammatory myopathy** (proximal weakness, elevated CK, ± rash of dermatomyositis) → rheumatology/neurology referral, consider underlying malignancy.\n- **Thyroid myopathy** → check TSH.\n- Treat the underlying cause; supportive care and follow-up.\n\n**Anchors for the rhabdo end of this branch:** rhabdomyolysis is conventionally **CK >5× ULN (~1,000 U/L)**; the classic triad of myalgia, weakness and dark urine is present in **<10%**. Fluid is the treatment — **lactated Ringer\'s is preferred over normal saline**, titrated to a **urine output of 1-3 mL/kg/h**. **Bicarbonate urine alkalinisation has not been shown to prevent AKI, ARF or dialysis** and is not routine. Details in [Rhabdomyolysis](#/tree/rhabdomyolysis).\n\n**⚠️ The statin trap — do not assume stopping the drug fixes it.** Ordinary statin myalgia and statin rhabdomyolysis resolve on withdrawal. **Statin-associated immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy (anti-HMGCR IMNM) does not** — it is an autoimmune myopathy triggered by the statin that **progresses after the statin is stopped**, and it needs immunosuppression, not reassurance. Clues: **progressive proximal weakness with a persistently high or rising CK weeks to months after statin withdrawal** (multinational cohort median peak CK ~7,000 U/L, range ~1,000-39,000), sometimes with dysphagia and respiratory involvement. Fewer than half normalise CK or power even with treatment. **If the CK does not fall after the statin is stopped, send anti-HMGCR antibodies and get neurology/rheumatology involved — do not keep calling it "statin myopathy."**',
