        title: 'Initial Bundle — Respiratory-Failure Watch',
        body: '**The acute-neuromuscular-weakness bundle centers on breathing (scale to acuity):**\n- **Bedside respiratory mechanics up front and serially:** single-breath count, **forced vital capacity (FVC)** and **negative inspiratory force (NIF/MIP)**. Warning thresholds: **FVC <20 mL/kg, NIF worse than −30 cmH₂O, MEP <40 cmH₂O ("20/30/40 rule")**, rapid decline, or weak cough/poor swallow.\n- **Intubate on trending mechanics and bulbar failure — do NOT wait for hypoxia or hypercapnia; SpO₂ and ABG fail late.** Anticipate NMBA pitfalls in MG.\n- **Stat potassium + ECG** on every acute weakness; correct dyskalemia.\n- **Pattern exam:** ascending vs descending, reflexes, pupils, fatigability, sensory level, UMN signs.\n- **Labs:** CK, electrolytes (K/Ca/PO₄/Mg), renal function, TSH; medication review; consider LP/NCS/MRI per suspected cause.\n- Cardiac/telemetry monitoring for autonomic instability (GBS).\n\n---\n\n**Where the 20/30/40 rule comes from, and what it is actually for.** The thresholds derive from **Lawn et al., Arch Neurol 2001** — a single-centre retrospective cohort of 114 GBS patients (1976-1996), in which VC <20 mL/kg, MIP worse than −30 cmH₂O and MEP <40 cmH₂O predicted the need for ventilation. It is a **prompt to escalate, prepare, and repeat the measurement**, not a stand-alone order to intubate. Two caveats the number alone hides:\n- **It should not be the sole trigger.** The clinical predictors carry as much weight: **bulbar/facial weakness, neck-flexor weakness, rapid progression (short onset-to-admission interval), and an ineffective cough.** In the same literature the commonly quoted *intubation* trigger is a **VC below ~15 mL/kg or an absolute VC under ~1 L** — tighter than the 20 mL/kg *warning* threshold. Escalating care at 20 and intubating around 15 is the usual reading; confirm against your own institutional protocol.\n- **Effort and seal are confounders.** Bulbar/facial weakness causes a mouth leak that makes the VC look falsely low; a poorly coached patient makes it look falsely high. Measure it yourself, twice, and **trust the trend across serial measurements over any single value.**\n\n**Bedside surrogates when you have no spirometer:**\n- **Single-breath count** — maximal inspiration, count aloud at a normal conversational cadence: **<20 is concerning, <10 signals imminent respiratory failure.**\n- **Inability to lift the head off the bed** (neck-flexor weakness) — the neck flexors and the diaphragm fail together, so this is a proxy for the diaphragm you cannot see.\n- **Inability to count to 20 in one breath, a wet or absent cough, pooling secretions, staccato speech, or a rising respiratory rate with paradoxical abdominal motion.**\n\n**⚠️ Why the pulse oximeter and the blood gas betray you here.** This is a *pump* failure, not a *gas-exchange* failure. The lung parenchyma is normal, so oxygenation is preserved until the very end — and if the patient is on any supplemental oxygen, **SpO₂ can be 100% minutes before arrest**. CO₂ likewise stays normal while the patient compensates with tachypnoea; by the time the PaCO₂ is frankly elevated the respiratory muscles have already decompensated. **Hypercapnia in neuromuscular weakness is a preterminal finding, not an early warning.** Intubate on the trend in mechanics and on bulbar failure.\n\n**⚠️ Non-invasive ventilation is not a uniform answer — it diverges by diagnosis.**\n- **Myasthenic crisis:** a **BiPAP trial before hypercapnia develops** can avert intubation and shorten ventilation. The **only** predictor of BiPAP failure in the Mayo series was **PaCO₂ >45 mmHg at initiation**. Requires an intact cough/gag and no significant bulbar weakness.\n- **GBS:** NIV is generally **not** recommended. Deterioration is poorly rescued by BiPAP (abrupt failures requiring emergency intubation are reported), the trajectory is weeks not hours, and bulbar weakness with a poor cough makes NIV an aspiration hazard.\n- **In either, significant bulbar dysfunction is a contraindication**, and NIV must never be used to delay an intubation that already meets criteria.\n\n**⚠️ Airway drugs — avoid succinylcholine in this whole population.** Subacute denervation and prolonged immobility up-regulate extrajunctional and α7 acetylcholine receptors across the entire muscle membrane; succinylcholine depolarises all of them and can cause **hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest**. Risk is generally taken to begin around **48-72 h** after the insult and to persist for months. **Use rocuronium.** Dose notes by branch:\n- **GBS, botulism, tick paralysis, critical-illness weakness, spinal cord injury:** rocuronium at a full RSI dose (**~1.2 mg/kg**); succinylcholine contraindicated.\n- **Myasthenia gravis:** the mirror image — MG patients are **resistant** to succinylcholine and **markedly sensitive** to non-depolarisers; rocuronium at roughly **1/3 to 1/2** the usual dose, with **sugammadex** available.\n- **LEMS:** exquisitely sensitive to **both** classes; expect prolonged paralysis and plan for it.',
